Colour model used by Photographic Studio By Bill (Develop > Old photo and film > Colourize)

ddcolor.npz    DDColor, "paper tiny" (ConvNeXt-T encoder), as exported to ONNX by Qualcomm AI Hub

DDColor: Xiaoyang Kang, Tao Yang, Wenqi Ouyang, Peiran Ren, Lingzhi Li, Xuansong Xie, "DDColor: Towards
Photo-Realistic Image Colorization via Dual Decoders", ICCV 2023. Code and weights: https://github.com/piddnad/DDColor
(weights ddcolor_paper_tiny), licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0; a copy is in the file LICENSE in this
folder. Unless required by applicable law or agreed to in writing, the model is distributed on an "AS IS" BASIS,
WITHOUT WARRANTIES OR CONDITIONS OF ANY KIND.

Source of this file: Qualcomm AI Hub's ONNX export (ddcolor-onnx-float.zip, linked from
https://huggingface.co/qualcomm/DDColor, model card licence Apache-2.0; the export code is BSD-3-Clause).

What was changed: the ONNX graph and its weights were converted by tools/convert_onnx_model.py into one NumPy
file; weights of 4096 values or more are stored as 8-bit integers with one scale per output channel (the colours
it predicts differ from the float model's by 0.15-0.65 CIE Lab units on average). It is run by the Studio's own
NumPy code (studio/engine/local/onnxnp.py, GPL-3.0); ONNX Runtime is not used.
